        "original_id": "f3c9296e-8b8c-5c99-a0eb-ba3646b6784d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Wilhelm Steinitz, Johannes Zukertort\n- Events: 1886 world championship match\n- Roles: world chess champion, chess master, writer, chess theoretician\n- Topics: Development of the World Chess Championship\n\nFacts:\n- Before 1886, various chess players were acclaimed as world champions without a contest being defined in advance as a world championship.\n- In 1886, the match between Wilhelm Steinitz and Johannes Zukertort was the first contest defined in advance as being for the world chess championship.\n- Wilhelm Steinitz was born on May 17, 1836, and died on August 12, 1900.\n- Wilhelm Steinitz was an Austrian and later American chess master.\n- Wilhelm Steinitz was the first undisputed world chess champion from 1886 to 1894.\n- Wilhelm Steinitz was also a highly influential writer and chess theoretician.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "3897d490-1367-5608-8ab9-29883f7c6f63",
        "text": "Page: Development of the World Chess Championship. Sentence: From this time onwards various players were \"acclaimed\" as world champions, but the first contest that was defined \"in advance\" as being for the world championship was the match between Steinitz and Zukertort in 1886.\nPage: Wilhelm Steinitz. Sentence: Wilhelm (later William) Steinitz (May 17, 1836 – August 12, 1900) was an Austrian and later American chess master, and the first undisputed world chess champion, from 1886 to 1894.\nPage: Wilhelm Steinitz. Sentence: He was also a highly influential writer and chess theoretician.",
